$I=\int \frac{d x}{\sqrt{\sin ^3 x \cos (x-\alpha)}}\begin{aligned}
& =\int \frac{d x}{\sqrt{\sin ^3 x[\cos x \cos \alpha+\sin x \sin \alpha]}} \\
& =\int \frac{d x}{\sqrt{\sin ^4 x[\cot x \cos \alpha+\sin \alpha]}} \\
& =\int \frac{d x}{\sin ^2 x \sqrt{\cos x \cos \alpha+\sin \alpha}}
\end{aligned}$<br/>Let $\cot x \cos \alpha+\sin \alpha=t$
Differentiate w.r.t. $x,-\cos \alpha \operatorname{cosec}^2 x d x=d t\begin{aligned}
& \Rightarrow \mathrm{I}=\frac{1}{-\cos \alpha} \int \frac{1}{\sqrt{t}} d t \Rightarrow \mathrm{I}=\frac{-1}{\cos \alpha} 2 \sqrt{t}+\mathrm{C} \\
& \Rightarrow \mathrm{I}=\frac{-2}{\cos \alpha} \sqrt{\cot x \cos \alpha+\sin \alpha}+\mathrm{C} \\
& \mathrm{I}=\frac{-2}{\sqrt{\cos \alpha}} \sqrt{\cot x+\tan \alpha}+\mathrm{C}
\end{aligned}$

$I=\int \frac{e^{2 x}}{\sqrt[4]{e^x+1}} d x$
Let $\left(e^x+1\right)^{\frac{1}{4}}=t \Rightarrow e^{2 x}=\left(t^4-1\right)^2$ Differentiating w.r.t. $x, 2 e^{2 \mathrm{x}} d x=2\left(t^4-1\right) 4 t^3 d t\begin{aligned}
& \therefore \mathrm{I}=\int \frac{\left(t^4-1\right)\left(4 t^3\right) d t}{t} \Rightarrow \mathrm{I}=4 \int\left(t^4-1\right)\left(t^2\right) d t \\
& \Rightarrow \mathrm{I}=4 \int\left(t^6-t^2\right) d t \Rightarrow \mathrm{I}=4\left[\frac{t^7}{7}-\frac{t^3}{3}\right]+\mathrm{C}
\end{aligned}\begin{aligned} & \Rightarrow \mathrm{I}=4 t^3\left[\frac{t^4}{7}-\frac{1}{3}\right]+\mathrm{C} \\ & \Rightarrow \mathrm{I}=4\left(e^x+1\right)^{\frac{3}{4}}\left[\frac{\left(e^x+1\right)}{7}-\frac{1}{3}\right]+\mathrm{C} \\ & \Rightarrow \mathrm{I}=\frac{4}{21}\left(e^x+1\right)^{\frac{3}{4}}\left(3 e^x-4\right)+\mathrm{C}\end{aligned}$

